{
  "lot_event": {
    "__comment": "[TRACE FIXTURE] Lot split/merge/track_in event log -- the ONLY source of lineage. One SPLIT or MERGE writes TWO rows (user 2026-08-01): the parent row carries child_lot with parent_lot BLANK, the child row carries parent_lot with child_lot BLANK; the pair is matched by the (parent, child) id pair, there is no event_id on the source. | The user sketched the absent side as '-'; it is stored BLANK instead, and that is not cosmetic. graph_materializer.compose_identity returns None for a blank part and the edge is skipped (graph_materializer.py:171-172), whereas a literal '-' is a perfectly good identity -- it would mint one Lot('-') node and wire EVERY split and merge in the fixture to it, producing a hub that makes any two unrelated lots look two hops apart. | slot_numbers and wafer_ids are colon-delimited and CORRESPOND POSITIONALLY with equal length; the generator asserts this itself rather than leaving it to the oracle, because a length mismatch does not fail loudly -- it silently reattributes wafers to the wrong slots. | event_time is 'string', not a date type: ISO 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' sorts correctly lexicographically, and a date cast that raises would drop the row that carries the ordering. The ORDER is what makes a reconstruction a finding rather than a guess.",
    "business_key": "event_id",
    "composite_key_source": ["lot", "event_type", "event_time"],
    "composite_key_separator": "|",
    "column_types": {
      "event_id": "string",
      "lot": "string",
      "event_type": "string",
      "parent_lot": "string",
      "child_lot": "string",
      "slot_numbers": "string",
      "wafer_ids": "string",
      "equipment": "string",
      "event_time": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "event_id",
      "lot",
      "event_type",
      "parent_lot",
      "child_lot",
      "slot_numbers",
      "wafer_ids",
      "equipment",
      "event_time"
    ]
  },
  "core_wafer_map": {
    "__comment": "[TRACE FIXTURE] Core wafer die map -- one row per die. c_bn is the bin (real column name, user 2026-08-01). | core_slot is 'string' ON PURPOSE: a 'number' declaration stores '07' as 7 and the leading zero is meaningful here (some sources emit '7'; that notation drift is deliberate fixture content, and it can only be studied if the storage preserves what arrived). | core_x/core_y are recorded in an UNKNOWN one of 8 frames (4 rotations x 2 flips), drawn independently of dt_x/dt_y. They are not the same unknown -- a row holding both couples them without fixing either. | wafer_id is deliberately sparse: absence is the enrichment work item.",
    "business_key": "core_cell_key",
    "composite_key_source": ["core_lot", "core_slot", "core_x", "core_y"],
    "composite_key_separator": "_",
    "column_types": {
      "core_cell_key": "string",
      "core_lot": "string",
      "core_slot": "string",
      "core_x": "number",
      "core_y": "number",
      "c_bn": "string",
      "wafer_id": "string",
      "event_time": "string"
    },
    "display_columns": [
      "core_cell_key",
      "core_lot",
      "core_slot",
      "core_x",
      "core_y",
      "c_bn",
      "wafer_id",
      "event_time"
    ],
    "__map_key_columns_comment": "R3 (SCHEMA_CANON): map_key_columns must be a SUBSET of composite_key_source. This said [wafer_id], which is not key material here -- and this declaration's own opening __comment says wafer_id is DELIBERATELY SPARSE ('absence is the enrichment work item'). A purge scoped by a sparse non-key column scopes by nothing: replace_map returns 200 and deletes no rows for every row that has not been enriched yet. The live config has always carried the correct value; only this .sample was wrong, so a FRESH DEPLOYMENT was the only thing that would ever have got the broken declaration. Repaired 2026-08-13 from the live config, R3 re-run across every table in both files afterwards: 0 violations.",
    "map_key_columns": ["core_lot", "core_slot"]
  },
  "core_usage_map": {
    "__comment": "DERIVED usage overlay keyed only by enriched core_wafer. dt_log remains the usage record and core_wafer_map remains physical defect/bin truth. Rows with lot/slot only are deliberately held until enrichment supplies wafer ID; otherwise the same physical wafer would split into two replace scopes. core_lot_slots is display provenance, not identity.",
    "business_key": "core_usage_cell_key",
    "composite_key_source": ["core_wafer", "core_x", "core_y"],
